Dashboard temperature-row geometry + narrow scrollbars (2026-07-31)
==================================================================

Problem (user, repeated): on the 320x240 main dashboard the temperature rows
wasted horizontal space -- a ~36px dead gap between each row's icon and its
value, another ~28px between the values and the graph, leaving the graph only
160px of a 320px screen.

Root cause (measured on-device with a temporary get_preferred_width() /
get_allocation() dump, not guessed): the device list lives in a
CustomScrolledWindow whose scrollbar was non-overlay, so GTK3 added the
scrollbar's 35px minimum width to the scrolled window's own minimum EVEN WHILE
THE BAR WAS HIDDEN. The left panel is pinned to that minimum
(set_hexpand(False)), so the 33 surplus px could not go anywhere useful -- and
because KlippyGtk.Button() builds both the icon button and the value button
with hexpand=True, Gtk.Grid handed the surplus out as extra COLUMN width
(+18px to the icon column, +16px to the value column). That is why a scrollbar
reserve sitting at the panel's RIGHT edge was responsible for BOTH visible
gaps, including the one between the icon and its value.

Measured, before -> after:
  scroll / left_panel min width ....... 108px -> 75px
  icon column (col 0) allocation ....... 47px -> 30px  (needs 29px)
  value column (col 1) allocation ...... 56px -> 40px  (needs 37px)
  gap icon -> value text ............... ~36px -> ~10px
  gap value text -> graph .............. ~28px -> ~20px (14px of which is
                                          main_menu's own column_spacing)
  graph_panel ...................... x158 w160 -> x125 w193  (+33px, +21%)

The device-wide half of the fix is PENDING-scroll-overlay-default.patch
(overlay_scrolling default False -> True in ks_includes/widgets/scroll.py).
This patch adds:
  * an explicit set_overlay_scrolling(True) on the dashboard's own scroll, so
    the dashboard cannot silently regress if that global default is reverted;
  * base.css: scrollbar slider 2.5em -> .5em (and the .heater-list override
    1.5em -> .5em, which would otherwise have become the WIDEST bar on the
    device instead of the narrowest);
  * base.css: undershoot.top/.bottom restored from upstream's
    `background-image: none` to a thin 2px accent line. Overlay scrollbars
    fade out ~1s after scrolling stops and GTK3 does that with
    gtk_widget_set_opacity()/gtk_widget_hide() on the scrollbar widget, which
    CSS cannot override -- so undershoot is the only persistent "there is more
    content below" cue left on a touchless D-pad device. Note undershoot draws
    per edge, only while the view is scrolled away from that edge: at the top
    of an overflowing list GTK3 draws undershoot.bottom (so "more below" is
    covered from the very first frame), and a list that fits draws nothing at
    all, which is the correct behaviour -- it is an overflow cue, not a
    scrollbar substitute. Verified live on the KlipperScreen Settings panel:
    narrow bar visible while scrolling, bar gone at rest, accent lines present
    at both edges at rest.

Theme coverage: every change is in base.css, which both themes load. Verified
statically that neither styles/big-font/style.css nor styles/big-font-light/
style.css contains any `scrollbar`, `undershoot` or (for big-font) any
`@define-color` rule at all -- the dark theme resolves entirely through
base.css's palette, where @active #404E57 contrasts cleanly against @bg
#13181C. So day and night are identical by construction; no theme file needed
editing and none was edited.

base.css has no standalone mirror under printer-configs/; this patch file is
its repo record.

CHAIN NOTE: the undershoot hunk below draws the line in @active at 2px. That
was superseded the same day -- in the light theme @active (#8FB6D1) on @bg
(#EDEFF2) turned out to be effectively invisible, which is why agent-D still
saw gcode_macros.py "look complete at rest" after this landed. The colour and
weight are re-tuned to alpha(@text, .45) at 3px in
button-padding-trim-and-undershoot-contrast.patch. Apply that one AFTER this
one; the two together match what is on the device.

